<h2>The dispute</h2>
<p>The parties married in 2012; their son was born in March 2021. The wife left the matrimonial home in August 2023 with the child. The husband sued for restitution of conjugal rights under Section 9 of the Hindu Marriage Act, 1955, and within that suit applied under Section 26 for temporary custody, a daily visitation schedule, video-call access and an order restraining the mother from obstructing contact. He alleged denial of all access; she answered that he had been abusive, irresponsible and had paid no maintenance, and that the child's welfare lay with her.</p>
<p>The Family Court at Ranchi refused temporary custody in September 2024, granting the father monthly supervised visits of two hours and bi-weekly video calls. He appealed under Section 19(1) of the Family Courts Act, 1984.</p>

<h2>The Division Bench's approach</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>The governing test.</strong> Following the line of authority from Gaurav Nagpal through Nil Ratan Kundu, Gaytri Bajaj and Yashita Sahu, the Bench treated the welfare and best interests of the child as the paramount consideration, before which statutory guardianship yields.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Stability for a child of tender years.</strong> A boy of about three and a half, settled with his mother since the separation, would be harmed by disruption; continuity of care, routine and schooling weighed decisively.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>No perversity below.</strong> The Family Court had weighed the material and chosen a conventional, welfare-centred arrangement; first-appellate interference requires perversity or illegality, and none appeared.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Contact preserved and enforced.</strong> The Bench stressed that the non-custodial parent must retain meaningful contact, and directed that visitation be facilitated under the supervision of the Jharkhand State Legal Services Authority, operationalised through the District Legal Services Authority.</div>
</div>
<div class="note">
<p>The supervision direction is the judgment's most practice-relevant feature. Visitation orders fail most often at the point of logistics — venue, handover, mistrust between parents. Routing implementation through the Legal Services Authority gives both sides a neutral facilitator and the court a reporting channel, without criminalising every missed meeting.</p>
</div>

<h2>Interim custody doctrine in brief</h2>
<p>Indian courts approach interim custody with two settled instincts. The first is restraint: interim orders should preserve, not experiment — a child's residence is not changed at the interlocutory stage unless the present arrangement threatens the child. The second is balance: the parent without custody is not the losing party; visitation is the child's right to both parents as much as the parent's right to the child, and modern orders increasingly specify video contact alongside physical meetings, as the Supreme Court encouraged in Yashita Sahu.</p>
<p>For fathers, the judgment is candid about the route that remains open: an interim refusal decides nothing final. Custody can be revisited as the child grows, on evidence of changed circumstances, and conduct during the interim period — regular visits, punctual maintenance, restraint in litigation — is itself the best evidence a parent can later produce.</p>

<h2>Practical pointers</h2>
<div class="check">
<p>Frame interim custody applications around the child's present routine and needs, not the parents' grievances; courts read the application for its centre of gravity.</p>
<p>Ask for visitation mechanics in detail — venue, duration, handover, video schedule — and, where cooperation is poor, for supervision through the DLSA or a court-identified facilitator.</p>
<p>Comply scrupulously with interim arrangements; a record of honoured visits and paid maintenance is the strongest material for any future variation.</p>
<p>Keep the child out of the litigation: courts notice, and weigh, which parent shields the child and which deploys him.</p>
</div>
